--TEST--
`Core\Http\Socket::receive` answers a message whose `text` and `bytes` are both `tainted`, so a plain binding of either is refused — `rule:security/tainted-sources`, `rule:http-server/an-outbound-socket-is-opened-like-an-outbound-call`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Agreement, asked of both of a message's payload readers: one mark on both,
// and the refusal is the only thing that can pin it. A plain value is
// assignable into a `tainted` target, so a case that bound the readers the
// other way round would pass over a member that had dropped the mark.
//
// Frames off a socket are input in the same sense a reply's body is: pinning an
// address settles which host the octets came from and says nothing about what
// is in them, and a conversation a program opened is no safer than one it was
// handed.
Core\Test::answerSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/feed", ["a text frame", "AB" as bytes]);

var $socket = Core\Http\Client::openSocket("wss://gateway.example.com/feed");
var $message = $socket->receive();

// Accepted: the qualifier is what the value is, and this is where it can be
// held.
tainted string $held = $message?->text() ?? ("" as tainted string);
echo $held, "\n";

// Refused, both of them: narrowing through an assignment is the one direction
// the qualifier never goes, and there is no generic launderer to put in front
// of it. The default carries the mark too, so what each binding is refused for
// is the qualifier and not a union of the two spellings.
string $text = $message?->text() ?? ("" as tainted string);
bytes $octets = $message?->bytes() ?? ("" as tainted bytes);
echo $text, Core\Bytes::length($octets), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
